Part 6 — Kernel Management
6.1 — View Installed Kernels

bash
# List all installed kernels
rpm -qa kernel

# See which kernel is currently running
uname -r

# Detailed kernel info
uname -a

# List available kernels in GRUB
sudo grubby --info=ALL | grep -E "^kernel|^title"
6.2 — Install / Update Kernel

bash
# Update kernel (keeps old kernels as fallback!)
sudo dnf update kernel

# Check how many kernels are retained
grep installonly_limit /etc/dnf/dnf.conf
# Default is 3 — meaning 3 kernels are kept
After a kernel update, GRUB automatically gets a new entry. You can choose the old kernel at boot if the new one causes issues.

6.3 — Set Default Kernel with grubby

bash
# List all kernels
sudo grubby --info=ALL

# Set a specific kernel as default
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-5.14.0-362.8.1.el9_3.x86_64

# Verify
sudo grubby --default-kernel
